    <descript>
      <abstract>This data release contains small uncrewed aircraft system (sUAS) products acquired from Sulphur Creek near Wilbur Hot Springs in Colusa County, California. The dataset includes: (1) high-resolution visible light orthomosaics, (2) a digital surface model (DSM) derived from structure-from-motion photogrammetry using visible imagery, (3) a raw thermal orthomosaic, (4) a calibrated thermal orthomosaic, (5) shapefile datasets with flight boundaries and control points. Thermal data were calibrated using in-field temperature control points acquired concurrently with flight operations. These datasets support characterization of geothermal surface expressions, stream-temperature anomalies, and associated landscape features. Products are provided in GeoTIFF format with associated metadata. This data release enables spatial analysis of surface temperature variations and topographic context, and may be useful for hydrologic, geothermal, and ecological studies in the region.</abstract>
      <purpose>The sUAS surveys at Sulphur Creek near Wilbur Hot Springs were conducted to identify and characterize zones of elevated surface temperature associated with geothermal fluid discharge. This area is known for active hydrothermal activity along a fault-controlled drainage, and the data support efforts to better understand the spatial distribution of thermal anomalies, their relationship to geologic structures, and their potential implications for geothermal resource assessment here, and by analogy, other areas experiencing active transpression. The combined visible and thermal datasets also provide baseline information for ongoing monitoring of geothermal features and may aid in the detection of changes in thermal expression over time.</purpose>
    </descript>
